</person-group><article-title>Effects of temperature, solid content and pH on the stability of black carrot anthocyanins</article-title><source>Food Chem.</source><year>2007</year><volume>101</volume><fpage>212</fpage><lpage>218</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.foodchem.2006.01.019</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig position="float" id="molecules-26-07000-f001" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Absorption spectrum scanning (<bold>A</bold>) and the percent of absorbance decay (<bold>B</bold>) for the anthocyanin extracts from blue flowers of butterfly pea and four other common fruits and vegetables. The wavelength value in the bracket indicates the λmax in (<bold>A</bold>) and the measurement wavelength in (<bold>B</bold>) for each plant material.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="molecules-26-07000-g001.jpg"><?image-name molecules-26-07000-g001.jpg?><?image-size 44534?><?image-md5 e3f104434c8e444f78f449fda099b7fc?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 442?><?image-original-width 1374?><?image-scaled-height 221?><?image-scaled-width 687?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/f6d3/8622631/e3f104434c8e/molecules-26-07000-g001.jpg?><?thumb-name molecules-26-07000-g001.gif?><?thumb-size 4891?><?thumb-md5 538fd933fbc379f81d8ca670003f0ec6?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 64?><?thumb-scaled-width 199?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/f6d3/8622631/538fd933fbc3/molecules-26-07000-g001.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="molecules-26-07000-f002" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Color and absorption spectra of CTAEs at different pHs. (<bold>A</bold>) Color of CTAEs from pH 0.5 to 13. (<bold>B</bold>) Absorption spectra of CTAEs from pH 0.5 to 13. The absorption peaks are highlighted by vertical lines with values of λmax presented.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="molecules-26-07000-g002.jpg"><?image-name molecules-26-07000-g002.jpg?><?image-size 85413?><?image-md5 65c72b0122698f24030dcf5200e458b7?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 742?><?image-original-width 841?><?image-scaled-height 494?><?image-scaled-width 560?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/f6d3/8622631/65c72b012269/molecules-26-07000-g002.jpg?><?thumb-name molecules-26-07000-g002.gif?><?thumb-size 5868?><?thumb-md5 2d190706de47e13c342861d8e7fc056f?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 88?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/f6d3/8622631/2d190706de47/molecules-26-07000-g002.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="molecules-26-07000-f003" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Speculated structural transformation of ternatins with pH variations. In the structure diagrams, D-glucose is simplified as Glu. The inset table shows the side chain variations of the 15 ternatins (A1–A3, B1–B4, C1–C5, and D1–D3) according to Terahara et al. [<xref rid="B16-molecules-26-07000" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>,<xref rid="B17-molecules-26-07000" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>,<xref rid="B18-molecules-26-07000" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>], in which G is D-glucose and C is <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>-coumaric acid. The 3′ and 5′ side chains are simplified as wavy lines in anthocyanin forms other than AH<sup>+</sup>. The blue and yellow arrows show the two transformation directions for AH<sup>+</sup>, i.e., deprotonation and hydration, respectively. Speculated pH range for each anthocyanin forms were shown. However, as CTAEs never displayed colorless, a question mark was used for the unclear pH range for the colorless hemiketal form.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="molecules-26-07000-g003.jpg"><?image-name molecules-26-07000-g003.jpg?><?image-size 96744?><?image-md5 6d6f260c3842a81aeef12c26ac71aa99?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 918?><?image-original-width 974?><?image-scaled-height 612?><?image-scaled-width 649?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/f6d3/8622631/6d6f260c3842/molecules-26-07000-g003.jpg?><?thumb-name molecules-26-07000-g003.gif?><?thumb-size 4957?><?thumb-md5 7d89b05add44ca861ce59a402e8d06c4?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 94?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/f6d3/8622631/7d89b05add44/molecules-26-07000-g003.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="molecules-26-07000-f004" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Combined effects of pH and temperature on the stability of CTAEs. (<bold>A</bold>) Color of CTAEs after 65 days’ storage in dark at 4, 25, 37, and 50 °C, respectively. Below is the color reference by universal pH indicator paper. (<bold>B</bold>) Absorbance decay percentages of CTAEs at pH 0.5 (λmax at 548 nm), pH 7 (λmax at 576 nm and 622 nm) and pH 10 (λmax at 628 nm) during 27 days’ storage in dark at 4, 25, 37, and 50 °C.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="molecules-26-07000-g004.jpg"><?image-name molecules-26-07000-g004.jpg?><?image-size 125699?><?image-md5 667a060c0150183abd0fb0484b885b52?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 841?><?image-original-width 743?><?image-scaled-height 841?><?image-scaled-width 743?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/f6d3/8622631/667a060c0150/molecules-26-07000-g004.jpg?><?thumb-name molecules-26-07000-g004.gif?><?thumb-size 7520?><?thumb-md5 b98fb636990a61242b809b6f4edf9f5e?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 113?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/f6d3/8622631/b98fb636990a/molecules-26-07000-g004.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="molecules-26-07000-f005" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>DPPH radical scavenging percentage for extracts of <italic toggle="yes">C. ternatea</italic> flowers at different pHs.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="molecules-26-07000-g005.jpg"><?image-name molecules-26-07000-g005.jpg?><?image-size 45756?><?image-md5 5f008dc08a0dc525d35c77c21ca43c17?><?image-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?image-original-height 384?><?image-original-width 789?><?image-scaled-height 384?><?image-scaled-width 789?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/f6d3/8622631/5f008dc08a0d/molecules-26-07000-g005.jpg?><?thumb-name molecules-26-07000-g005.gif?><?thumb-size 4792?><?thumb-md5 5786651a2154c0269e537a173be6d60c?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 80?><?thumb-scaled-width 164?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/f6d3/8622631/5786651a2154/molecules-26-07000-g005.gif?></graphic></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="molecules-26-07000-t001" orientation="portrait"><object-id pub-id-type="pii">molecules-26-07000-t001_Table 1</object-id><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Kinetic parameters of thermal degradation for CTAEs at different combinations of temperatures and pHs.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-top:solid thin;border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Temperature (°C)</th><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-top:solid thin;border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">R<sup>2</sup></th><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-top:solid thin;border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">k (d<sup>−1</sup>)</th><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-top:solid thin;border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">k (min<sup>−1</sup>)</th><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-top:solid thin;border-bo